---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How ClickTrail works — capture, merge, convert"
  description="Capture UTMs and click IDs on landing, merge first and last touch into one flat payload, and send conversions with the full trail. Deterministic."
  canonicalPath="/how-it-works/"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep">/</span><span aria-current="page">How it works</span>
      </nav>
      <p class="kicker">Pipeline deep-dive</p>
      <h1>From ad click to conversion, deterministically</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Three stages — capture, merge, convert — run on a pure engine where
        time, IDs, storage, consent, and network are injected by callers,
        never requested.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h2>1. Capture</h2>
      <p>
        When a visitor lands from an ad or campaign, the engine parses the
        landing URL into an attribution touch: UTMs
        (<code>utm_source</code>, <code>utm_medium</code>,
        <code>utm_campaign</code>, <code>utm_content</code>,
        <code>utm_term</code>), ad click IDs (<code>gclid</code>,
        <code>gbraid</code>, <code>wbraid</code>, <code>fbclid</code>,
        <code>msclkid</code>, <code>ttclid</code>), and referrer
        classification with channel labels.
      </p>
      <p>
        Parsing happens wherever your stack runs it — in the browser adapter,
        in SSR middleware (Qwik, SvelteKit, Django, Flask), or as a pure
        function call in Node, a worker, or a test. The context persists in
        your own first-party storage under your own domain.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Merge</h2>
      <p>
        Every navigation re-parses observed context through
        <code>mergeAttributionTouch</code>: the <strong>first touch is
        preserved</strong> (<code>ft_*</code> fields are write-once) while the
        <strong>last touch is updated</strong> (<code>lt_*</code> fields
        refresh). Both collapse into one flat canonical payload stamped with
        <code>schema_version</code> and <code>classifier_version</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because the payload is flat &mdash; no nested vendor objects &mdash;
        you can store it directly in your own database, map it into a CRM, or
        forward it through GTM's <code>dataLayer</code>. The richer
        <code>marketing_trail</code> envelope stays attached for consumers
        that want it; it is additive and never required.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Convert</h2>
      <p>
        Lead, booking, sale, and refund events carry the full trail:
        attribution fields, the click-IDs map, consent state, and form
        context ride along on every conversion. Server helpers ship with each
        integration so conversions can be sent from trusted server code
        (form actions, route actions, hooks) instead of the browser.
      </p>

      <h2>Determinism: golden fixtures ARE the spec</h2>
      <p>
        The core engine is pure: same inputs produce the same output. That is
        what makes golden-fixture replay testing possible. Golden fixtures
        captured from the live WordPress plugin are replayed in CI to verify
        parity between this engine and the plugin distribution. Where
        fixtures disagree with docs, fixtures win and docs get fixed.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Classifier behavior changes are <strong>major semver, by definition</strong>.</li>
        <li>Every payload carries <code>schema_version</code> and <code>classifier_version</code>.</li>
        <li>Time, IDs, storage, consent, and network are injected &mdash; so any scenario is reproducible in a test.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Idempotency: mint once, deliver at-most-once</h2>
      <p>
        Every event carries an <code>event_id</code> (prefixed UUID-style,
        <code>evt_</code>) generated by the shared core id generation &mdash;
        never by the platform adapter. Retryable senders reuse the same
        <code>event_id</code> across retries of the same logical occurrence:
        n8n retries, webhook redeliveries, double booking callbacks.
      </p>
      <p>
        Collectors treat duplicate <code>event_id</code> values within the
        retention window as success-no-op &mdash; at-most-once delivery
        semantics end to end. For server replays the core ships a
        deterministic derivation <code>(site_id, external_key)</code> &rarr;
        stable <code>event_id</code>, e.g. booking callbacks keyed by
        <code>${'{'}booking_id{'}'}:${'{'}stage{'}'}</code>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Ready to plug this into your stack? Browse the
        <a href="/integrations/">integrations hub</a> or read the
        <a href="/event-contract/">canonical event contract</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
